---
description: How to add or change an Android app in this monorepo.
alwaysApply: true
---

Android apps are top-level folders, built in CI and published as a sideload APK plus a landing page. Follow this whenever you create or change one.

## Project

1. One app per top-level folder. Put `SPEC.md` in that folder (see the spec rule).
2. Kotlin and Jetpack Compose. Package and `applicationId`: `com.tepmex.<compactname>` (lowercase, no hyphens).
3. Toolchain, matching the current apps in CloudAgenticCoding:
   - Android Gradle Plugin `8.9.2`
   - Kotlin `2.1.21` and `org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.compose` `2.1.21`
   - Gradle wrapper `8.11.1`
   - JVM / `jvmTarget` 17
   - `compileSdk` and `targetSdk` 36
   - `minSdk` 34 unless the task explicitly needs an older device
4. Release builds: `isMinifyEnabled = true`, `isShrinkResources = true`, default optimize ProGuard file plus `proguard-rules.pro`.

## Signing

Every app is signed with the shared sideload key so a later APK installs over the previous one and keeps app data.

1. Copy `android/sideload.keystore` and `android/sideload-signing.properties` into the app directory. Do not generate a new keystore.
2. In `app/build.gradle.kts`, before the `android {}` block:

```kotlin
extra["sideloadPropertyPrefix"] = "compactname"
apply(from = rootProject.file("../android/sideload-signing.gradle.kts"))

val autoVersionCode: Int = extra["autoVersionCode"] as Int
val useCustomSigning: Boolean = extra["useCustomSigning"] as Boolean
```

3. `versionCode = autoVersionCode` and `versionName = "1.0.$autoVersionCode"`. The shared script sets `autoVersionCode` from the unix time, so each build sorts after the previous one.
4. Add a `sideload` signing config when `useCustomSigning` is true, and use it for both `debug` and `release`. Otherwise fall back to the debug config.
5. `sideloadPropertyPrefix` is lowercase letters only. Optional local override keys in `local.properties`: `<prefix>.signingStoreFile`, `<prefix>.signingStorePassword`, `<prefix>.signingKeyAlias`, `<prefix>.signingKeyPassword`.

CI rejects an APK whose certificate is not `android/expected-sideload-cert-sha256.txt`.

## Landing page

`site/index.html` is the GitHub Pages page for the APK.

- Download link: `<a class="download" href="<folder>.apk">`.
- Include a closed marker pair so the deploy footer can be replaced:

```html
<!-- deploy-footer -->
<!-- deploy-footer -->
```

- Commit `site/styles.css` as a copy of `android/landing/styles.css` for local preview. Publish overwrites it with `android/landing/styles.css`.

## Catalog

Append the folder name to `ANDROID_APPS` in `scripts/ci-apps.sh`. That is the only app list; do not hardcode it in `.github/workflows/deploy.yml`.

A WebView (or other) wrapper around a web app in this repo also:

- implements `<app>/scripts/sync-web-assets.sh` (CI runs it before `assembleRelease` when the file exists)
- returns the wrapper name from `android_for_web` for that web folder
- returns `bun` or `node` from `android_web_runtime` so CI installs the runtime that the sync script needs

Changing `android/sideload-signing.gradle.kts`, the keystore, the properties, or the expected certificate rebuilds every Android app. Changing `android/landing/` or `scripts/inject-static-site-footer.ts` republishes landing pages without a Gradle build.
